WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.900
Film Courage: When you plan on shooting it
[Keith’s next film], what do you think you

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.550
will do differently?

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:12.639
What are some things that you learned from
say if a location that fell through, etc.?

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:15.042
Keith Sutliff, Filmmaker: Well, I would have
hired more people, I mean ideally.

00:00:15.042 --> 00:00:16.042
I think on the budget, I needed more people.

00:00:16.042 --> 00:00:17.042
Bigger crew, more people in pre-production
helping and in post production.

00:00:17.042 --> 00:00:18.042
So definitely more people.

00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:19.042
That’s really about it.

00:00:19.042 --> 00:00:20.042
The bigger the team, the bigger the army you
have, the easier it is for you as a producer

00:00:20.042 --> 00:00:21.042
on the film.

00:00:21.042 --> 00:00:22.042
So you’re not juggling too many hats.

00:00:22.042 --> 00:00:23.042
You have people helping out.

00:00:23.042 --> 00:00:25.880
So essentially just hiring more people and
delegating these duties and jobs to these

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:26.880
other people.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.860
Film Courage: Do you like to do a lot of stuff
yourself?

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.340
You know there are certain people, they love
to be really hands-on.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.340
Keith Sutliff: I do.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.340
I do.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.340
I did a lot of editing on this last one.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:37.340
I had an editor but I did a lot of editing
on THE MASON BROTHERS.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.340
My editor and I were writing it but I did
do a lot of editing on it.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.340
That was very tedious.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.340
I edited a lot of stuff like the trailers
and some little things, promotional stuff.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.590
I think editing is…I don’t say I hate
the most, but it’s very tedious and time

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:44.590
consuming out of all the things.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.699
That’s why I told my team “Hey this next
one, there’s no way.”

00:00:47.699 --> 00:00:52.039
I’ll edit maybe the beginnings and may help
out on the trailers but obviously I’ll let

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:53.039
someone edit the rest.

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:54.531
I’ll give them the notes on how I kind of
want it.

00:00:54.531 --> 00:00:56.920
On this last one I was doing like, maybe 35%
of the scenes I was editing and like the other

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.989
30-35%, I was telling my editor what I wanted
and stuff.

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:00.829
I let him edit like 35% and it was a lot of
dialogue because there were some complex scenes

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:05.120
in there, what were like bad scenes, something
like that where it had to be edited a certain

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.620
way I had in mind because I thought of my
shot list (what I came up with my cinematographer)

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.680
that just from studying films for so long,
it’s hard to explain to somebody like "Hey

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.570
when something bad happens in the movie, the
camera needs to flip to this angel over here

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.610
to show this reaction of the actors face and
hide the instrument” or whatever.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.970
Instead of explaining that, it’s kind of
easier just to do it because you’ve seen

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:25.310
it in films and how you want the shot that
you developed.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.100
So it was a lot of hands on with that, but
for the next one it’s too time-consuming.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.360
I’ll let someone just do it and I’ll correct
them with notes or whatever.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.270
Film Courage: What are you using to edit on?

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.270
Keith Sutliff: Adobe Premiere.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:36.270
It’s a good program.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.270
My editor had a big personal computer.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:38.270
I know a lot of people edit off of Macintosh
computers, but he had a huge PC station.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.270
That’s what we edited on and a lot of it
was done with Adobe.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:40.920
Film Courage: And you taught yourself how
to use Adobe Creative Suite [a.k.a Creative

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:41.920
Cloud]?

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.800
Keith Sutliff: Actually I learned it in my
filmmaking classes.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.800
We started editing on everything in Adobe.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.200
From all the things we shot we learned from
the computers in the lab how to edit from

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.200
the Professor.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.860
Alex Pickering is a great professor from USC
and he teaches the course in East LA.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.461
He was teaching how to edit and we just learned
from the courses and materials and the guides.

00:01:59.461 --> 00:02:00.810
They had these little CD-roms and kind of
walk you though stuff.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:05.170
That’s what I learned and I just went from
there.

